I'm not an expert on this topic but, instead of focusing on the chi-square calculation, focus on the solution--the parameter estimates. Programs differ in terms of things like starting value selection and convergence criteria. Historically, I think, LISREL was "tuned" to work best for a well-fitting model, while some packages were optimized for models with an approximate fit, so if your population includes lack of fit, that might induce differences. Check your parameter estimates at a high level of precision, and you may see differences, which would turn into a different Sigma-hat, which would naturally produce a different chi-square. Alternately, you might generate data so as to eliminate lack of fit and / or sampling variance. If you eliminate both of those, then I think differences between programs will disappear (unless maybe your sample size is very small).

